          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "Progress Notes",
              "title": "New Patient Preventive Visit",
              "author": "PCP",
              "date": "2023-08-14T08:00",
              "body": "43 y/o woman establishing care. Previous PCP in another town; records release signed today.\n\nPMH: iron deficiency anemia, picked up on routine bloodwork around 2019 per her account, on ferrous sulfate 325 once a day since. Takes it most mornings, tolerates it. Gallstones years ago, never had surgery, no attacks since. No other chronic problems.\nOB/GYN: G2P2, both vaginal deliveries (2010, 2013). Tubal ligation 2014. Cycles regular. Periods on the heavy side and crampy the first couple of days, which she says has been her pattern for years, no recent change. Uses ibuprofen 400-600 mg as needed during periods.\nMeds: ferrous sulfate 325 mg daily; ibuprofen OTC prn cramps.\nAllergies: NKDA.\nFHx: mother had adenomyosis, hysterectomy at 48. Sister with endometriosis. Father with type 2 diabetes.\nSocial: teaches high school math. Never smoker. Wine 1-2 glasses a week.\nPrevention: last Pap with HPV 2021 at the old office, normal per pt. Mammogram 2022, normal. Tetanus booster not recalled.\n\nROS: no dizziness, no shortness of breath on stairs, no palpitations. No change in bowels, no dark stools. Energy fine.\n\nExam: vitals as recorded. Well appearing, conjunctivae pink. Thyroid not enlarged. Heart RRR without murmur. Lungs clear. Abdomen soft, nontender, no organomegaly. No edema. Skin unremarkable. Breast and pelvic exams not repeated today given recent normal screening, she prefers to wait.\n\nA/P\n1. Iron deficiency anemia, chronic, on oral iron - CBC and iron studies today. Continue ferrous sulfate.\n2. History of gallstones - asymptomatic, nothing to do.\n3. Health maintenance - Tdap today. Screening mammogram ordered for the fall. CMP, lipids, TSH, A1c (father with T2DM), one-time HIV and hep C screening. Pap due 2026. PHQ-2 0.\n4. Records requested.\nReturn in a year, sooner as needed."
            }
          ]
